  "raw_response": "```python\ndef solve(grid):\n    rows = len(grid)\n    cols = len(grid[0]) if rows > 0 else 0\n    \n    # Find all non-zero cells and their values\n    non_zero = []\n    for r in range(rows):\n        for c in range(cols):\n            if grid[r][c] != 0:\n                non_zero.append((r, c, grid[r][c]))\n    \n    # Create output grid initialized with zeros\n    output = [[0] * cols for _ in range(rows)]\n    \n    # Process each pair: we need to find connected components\n    # Each pair consists of: a \"center\" (1 or 2) and \"markers\" of same value\n    # The markers indicate where to place the cross pattern around the center\n    \n    # Find centers (values 1 or 2) and their associated markers\n    centers = [(r, c, v) for (r, c, v) in non_zero if v in [1, 2]]\n    markers = [(r, c, v) for (r, c, v) in non_zero if v not in [1, 2]]\n    \n    for cr, cc, cv in centers:\n        # Find markers with the same value as this center's associated markers\n        # Look at examples: center value 1 pairs with marker value 7, center 2 pairs with 3\n        # Actually, let's check: in example 1, center 2 has marker 3, center 1 has marker 7\n        # In example 2, center 1 has marker 7, center 2 has marker 3\n        # In example 3, center 1 has marker 7, center 2 has marker 3\n        \n        # So 1 pairs with 7, 2 pairs with 3\n        target_marker = 7 if cv == 1 else 3\n        \n        # Find markers of this type that should form a cross with the center\n        # The markers indicate direction from center\n        associated_markers = [(r, c) for (r, c, v) in markers if v == target_marker]\n        \n        # Place the center\n        output[cr][cc] = cv\n        \n        # Place markers in cross pattern around center based on original marker positions\n        for mr, mc in associated_markers:\n            # Determine relative position\n            dr = mr - cr\n            dc = mc - cc\n            \n            # Normalize to get direction (only cardinal directions in examples)\n            if dr != 0 and dc != 0:\n                # Diagonal or other - check if it's meant to be cardinal\n                pass\n            \n            # Place marker in cross direction from center\n            if dr < 0 and dc == 0:  # above\n                output[cr-1][cc] = target_marker\n            elif dr > 0 and dc == 0:  # below\n                output[cr+1][cc] = target_marker\n            elif dc < 0 and dr == 0:  # left\n                output[cr][cc-1] = target_marker\n            elif dc > 0 and dr == 0:  # right\n                output[cr][cc+1] = target_marker\n            else:\n                # Handle case where marker indicates diagonal or other direction\n                # Normalize to nearest cardinal\n                if abs(dr) > abs(dc):\n                    if dr < 0:\n                        output[cr-1][cc] = target_marker\n                    else:\n                        output[cr+1][cc] = target_marker\n                else:\n                    if dc < 0:\n                        output[cr][cc-1] = target_marker\n                    else:\n                        output[cr][cc+1] = target_marker\n    \n    return output\n```",
  "script": "def solve(grid):\n    rows = len(grid)\n    cols = len(grid[0]) if rows > 0 else 0\n    \n    # Find all non-zero cells and their values\n    non_zero = []\n    for r in range(rows):\n        for c in range(cols):\n            if grid[r][c] != 0:\n                non_zero.append((r, c, grid[r][c]))\n    \n    # Create output grid initialized with zeros\n    output = [[0] * cols for _ in range(rows)]\n    \n    # Process each pair: we need to find connected components\n    # Each pair consists of: a \"center\" (1 or 2) and \"markers\" of same value\n    # The markers indicate where to place the cross pattern around the center\n    \n    # Find centers (values 1 or 2) and their associated markers\n    centers = [(r, c, v) for (r, c, v) in non_zero if v in [1, 2]]\n    markers = [(r, c, v) for (r, c, v) in non_zero if v not in [1, 2]]\n    \n    for cr, cc, cv in centers:\n        # Find markers with the same value as this center's associated markers\n        # Look at examples: center value 1 pairs with marker value 7, center 2 pairs with 3\n        # Actually, let's check: in example 1, center 2 has marker 3, center 1 has marker 7\n        # In example 2, center 1 has marker 7, center 2 has marker 3\n        # In example 3, center 1 has marker 7, center 2 has marker 3\n        \n        # So 1 pairs with 7, 2 pairs with 3\n        target_marker = 7 if cv == 1 else 3\n        \n        # Find markers of this type that should form a cross with the center\n        # The markers indicate direction from center\n        associated_markers = [(r, c) for (r, c, v) in markers if v == target_marker]\n        \n        # Place the center\n        output[cr][cc] = cv\n        \n        # Place markers in cross pattern around center based on original marker positions\n        for mr, mc in associated_markers:\n            # Determine relative position\n            dr = mr - cr\n            dc = mc - cc\n            \n            # Normalize to get direction (only cardinal directions in examples)\n            if dr != 0 and dc != 0:\n                # Diagonal or other - check if it's meant to be cardinal\n                pass\n            \n            # Place marker in cross direction from center\n            if dr < 0 and dc == 0:  # above\n                output[cr-1][cc] = target_marker\n            elif dr > 0 and dc == 0:  # below\n                output[cr+1][cc] = target_marker\n            elif dc < 0 and dr == 0:  # left\n                output[cr][cc-1] = target_marker\n            elif dc > 0 and dr == 0:  # right\n                output[cr][cc+1] = target_marker\n            else:\n                # Handle case where marker indicates diagonal or other direction\n                # Normalize to nearest cardinal\n                if abs(dr) > abs(dc):\n                    if dr < 0:\n                        output[cr-1][cc] = target_marker\n                    else:\n                        output[cr+1][cc] = target_marker\n                else:\n                    if dc < 0:\n                        output[cr][cc-1] = target_marker\n                    else:\n                        output[cr][cc+1] = target_marker\n    \n    return output",
